            "heading": "Okinawa Did Not Find The Fountain Of Youth. They Put It On The Dinner Table.",
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/red-algae-chopsticks.jpg",
            "image_alt": "Older Okinawan adults preparing a traditional seafood meal",
            "body": "<p>Okinawa has four times more people living past 100 than anywhere else in the world.</p><p>That kind of number is not explained by one lucky grandmother. It points to a culture that has been doing something differently for generations.</p><p>Their traditional meals are rich in salmon, shrimp, shellfish, seaweed, and other foods that carry astaxanthin, the red survival compound made by microalgae. Long before the rest of the world could pronounce it, Okinawans were eating it as part of daily life.</p><p>They did not swallow a cabinet full of pills. They kept feeding their cells a natural shield the modern Western diet quietly removed.</p>",
            "close_text": "When an entire island produces four times more 100-year-olds, it is worth asking what is on their plate that is missing from yours.",
            "show_inline_cta": false,
            "inline_cta_text": "",
            "inline_cta_label": "Put the King of Antioxidants on guard",
